@* TimelineEntry: one line of a delivery's append-only history (FR-105 to FR-108).

   A status change, a note, or both. The dot's glyph is the entry's own summary - Confirm for a
   delivery that arrived, Warning for one that did not, Edit for any other move, Chat for a line
   that only says something - so a reader scanning the column sees the shape of the history before
   reading a word of it.

   Entries are never edited or deleted, so there are no row actions here and there is no place to
   put one: that is the component saying what the record is, not an omission.

   The two statuses render as StatusLabels rather than as words, and both of them render, so the
   line reads as a move rather than as a destination. A note-only entry has neither and draws no
   arrow between nothing and nothing. *@

<li class="dt-timeline-entry">
    <span class="dt-timeline-dot" aria-hidden="true">
        <Icon Name="@Glyph" />
    </span>

    <div class="dt-timeline-line">
        @if (PreviousStatus is not null || NewStatus is not null)
        {
            <div class="dt-timeline-change">
                @if (PreviousStatus is { } previous)
                {
                    <DeliveryStatusLabel Status="previous" />
                }
                @if (PreviousStatus is not null && NewStatus is not null)
                {
                    <span class="dt-timeline-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                }
                @if (NewStatus is { } next)
                {
                    <DeliveryStatusLabel Status="next" />
                }
            </div>
        }

        <div class="dt-timeline-meta">
            @Actor
            <span class="dt-timeline-when">@When</span>
        </div>

        @* FR-106: a note rides on the entry it explains, whether or not that entry moved anything. *@
        @if (Note is not null)
        {
            <p class="dt-timeline-note">@Note</p>
        }
    </div>
</li>

@code {
    /// <summary>Where the delivery was before this entry, or null when the entry moved nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public DeliveryStatus? PreviousStatus { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where it went, or null when the entry moved nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public DeliveryStatus? NewStatus { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Who did it: the name when this viewer may be told it, the role badge either way. Supplied by
    /// the screen rather than assembled here, because the role words are literal localizer keys and
    /// LocalizationTests can only see the ones written in markup.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Actor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>When it happened, already formatted in the culture the screen is running in.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? When { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What was said about it, or null.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Note { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The dot's glyph, read off what the entry did rather than passed in: the mapping is the same
    /// on every timeline, and a parameter would be an invitation for one screen to disagree.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Read off both ends rather than off the destination alone. An entry that records where a
    /// delivery came from and not where it went still moved a status, and switching on
    /// <see cref="NewStatus"/> by itself drew it with the "somebody spoke" glyph - a move filed
    /// under conversation.
    /// </remarks>
    private IconName Glyph => (PreviousStatus, NewStatus) switch
    {
        (_, DeliveryStatus.Delivered) => IconName.Confirm,
        (_, DeliveryStatus.Failed) => IconName.Warning,

        // Nothing moved at either end: this is somebody speaking, which is the chat glyph.
        (null, null) => IconName.Chat,

        // Any other move: something changed, and the entry says what.
        _ => IconName.Edit,
    };
}
